for many it would be a dream come true  to work on cruise liners like this  Martin Cornelius is living that dream  he's been working as an apprentice at  the Maya valve shipyard since last  September from I get up at 5:00 a.m. and  right to work my bike I start at 6:30 we  meet in the workshop the foreman assigns  us to an area and off we go the 17 year  old could hardly wait to start his  apprenticeship the first two things he  learned wearing a helmet and protective  goggles is mandatory at all times and  secondly the shipyard is very big he  clocks up quite a few miles in a day  yeah I will walk through hall numbers  five and six almost to the end so I can  show you where I'm working in the block  assembly and what I do there when I wake  him talk  physical fitness is vital the holes are  hundreds of meters long workers at the  shipyard typically spend one year  building a cruise liner in these halls  Martin goes straight his Foreman he  hears that today's task is to install a  porthole in one of the blocks the two  then immediately get to work the  apprenticeship runs for three and a half  years Martin is in his second year of  training  he spent his first year at a vocational  College learning all the basics we learn  how the tools work in theory and then in  practice we learn about finally how to  work with a lathe you sawing milling and  other metalwork the kind of huge  passenger line is that the Maya shipyard  builds have to be constructed in stages  the work is divided into individual ship  blocks which are put together later the  construction mechanics play a key role  in all of this  and that's the job Martin is learning he  later be responsible for the interior  construction a multi-faceted job that  not everyone is suited to Martin's  training supervisor went through the  same apprenticeship himself 28 years ago  you need a basic School Leaving  Certificate with good grades especially  in math you need to be a team player and  your work ethic and social skills are  important so is your overall personality  martin caninus is one of 270 trainees  during the lunch break they all want to  know what work he was doing what the TV  crew was looking over his shoulder he  says he's spending the afternoon on  board the ship something not everyone  gets to do the workers have to line up  to be registered before they can board  the meijer shipyard specializes in  luxury cruise liners this one is nearly  ready the trainees earn nearly a  thousand euros a month so they couldn't  afford a Caribbean holiday on one of  these ships instead they have to focus  on working hard the shipyard is building  larger cruise liners these days and in  shorter time that means apprentices have  to cope with stress and working under  constant time pressure as the work  speeds up they need to keep pace it used  to take two years to build a ship of  this size no it's done in half that time  there's no shortage of work I'll  continue my training until my fourth  year just under two more years and then  hopefully get a job here the chances of  being taken on are pretty good the order  books are full soon the team will be  starting work on the next seagoing Giant
 

